WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:05.314
Day zero is a Wednesday.

2
00:00:05.314 --> 00:00:08.231
Seven days later, day seven

3
00:00:08.231 --> 00:00:10.544
it is Wednesday again.

4
00:00:10.544 --> 00:00:13.260
And again on day fourteen.

5
00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:16.324
Days that are a multiple of seven

6
00:00:16.406 --> 00:00:18.956
apart fall on the same weekday.

7
00:00:18.956 --> 00:00:21.998
So which weekday is day one hundred?

8
00:00:21.998 --> 00:00:23.890
Pause and work it out.

9
00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:30.541
One hundred minus two is ninety-eight

10
00:00:30.541 --> 00:00:33.935
and ninety-eight is seven times fourteen.

11
00:00:34.016 --> 00:00:36.521
So day one hundred falls on the

12
00:00:36.602 --> 00:00:39.350
same weekday as day two: a Friday.

13
00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:43.186
Same weekday is a relation between days

14
00:00:43.274 --> 00:00:45.888
a and b are related when seven

15
00:00:45.976 --> 00:00:47.544
divides a minus b.

16
00:00:47.632 --> 00:00:49.811
It treats day two and day

17
00:00:49.898 --> 00:00:51.990
one hundred as the same

18
00:00:51.990 --> 00:00:53.820
the way equals does.

19
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.710
A relation like that is called

20
00:00:56.787 --> 00:00:59.481
an equivalence relation: reflexive

21
00:00:59.481 --> 00:01:01.560
symmetric, and transitive.

22
00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:05.330
Here is same parity on one, two, three

23
00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:07.171
four, from part one.

24
00:01:07.171 --> 00:01:10.853
Its diagonal is full, so it is reflexive.

25
00:01:10.853 --> 00:01:13.308
It is its own mirror image

26
00:01:13.308 --> 00:01:15.062
so it is symmetric.

27
00:01:15.062 --> 00:01:16.991
And it is transitive

28
00:01:16.991 --> 00:01:20.147
one and three have the same parity

29
00:01:20.147 --> 00:01:23.830
three and one too, and so do one and one.

30
00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:26.033
Equals, same parity

31
00:01:26.125 --> 00:01:28.348
and same weekday are all

32
00:01:28.441 --> 00:01:30.479
equivalence relations.

33
00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:32.980
Less than or equal is not

34
00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:34.740
it fails symmetry.

35
00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.073
Careful with close to.

36
00:01:37.153 --> 00:01:40.261
Say two people are related if they live

37
00:01:40.341 --> 00:01:43.130
within one kilometre of each other.

38
00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:45.760
That is reflexive and symmetric.

39
00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.628
Ana lives zero point eight

40
00:01:48.725 --> 00:01:50.566
kilometres from Bo

41
00:01:50.566 --> 00:01:54.828
and Bo zero point eight kilometres from Cy.

42
00:01:54.925 --> 00:01:57.637
But Ana and Cy are one point

43
00:01:57.734 --> 00:01:59.768
six kilometres apart.

44
00:01:59.768 --> 00:02:01.317
Not transitive.

45
00:02:01.414 --> 00:02:04.030
Being close is not the same

46
00:02:04.127 --> 00:02:05.870
as being the same.

47
00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:10.055
Now the most important example.

48
00:02:10.171 --> 00:02:13.427
a is congruent to b modulo n

49
00:02:13.543 --> 00:02:16.450
when n divides a minus b.

50
00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:20.379
Twenty-three is congruent to two, mod seven

51
00:02:20.379 --> 00:02:22.657
the difference, twenty-one

52
00:02:22.657 --> 00:02:24.447
is seven times three.

53
00:02:24.447 --> 00:02:26.643
Negative numbers work too.

54
00:02:26.643 --> 00:02:30.141
Minus four is congruent to two, mod three

55
00:02:30.141 --> 00:02:33.720
because minus six is three times minus two.

56
00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:35.116
Theorem

57
00:02:35.116 --> 00:02:39.708
congruence mod n is an equivalence relation.

58
00:02:39.708 --> 00:02:42.973
Three proofs, one per property.

59
00:02:42.973 --> 00:02:46.033
Reflexive: a minus a is zero

60
00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:48.380
which is n times zero.

61
00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:53.065
Symmetric: if a minus b is n times k

62
00:02:53.065 --> 00:02:57.166
then b minus a is n times minus k

63
00:02:57.166 --> 00:03:00.330
and minus k is an integer.

64
00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:04.463
Transitive: say a minus b is n k

65
00:03:04.463 --> 00:03:06.985
and b minus c is n m.

66
00:03:06.985 --> 00:03:12.030
Take n equal to three, with five minus two

67
00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.552
and eight minus five.

68
00:03:14.552 --> 00:03:17.418
Add the two differences

69
00:03:17.418 --> 00:03:20.513
a minus c is n k plus n m

70
00:03:20.513 --> 00:03:23.609
which is n times k plus m.

71
00:03:23.609 --> 00:03:26.360
So a is congruent to c.

72
00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.406
An equivalence relation sorts

73
00:03:29.492 --> 00:03:31.107
a set into classes.

74
00:03:31.193 --> 00:03:33.574
The class of a is the set of

75
00:03:33.659 --> 00:03:35.700
everything related to a.

76
00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:39.188
Write zero to eleven in rows of three.

77
00:03:39.188 --> 00:03:42.904
Mod three, the class of zero is zero, three

78
00:03:42.904 --> 00:03:45.299
six, nine: the first column.

79
00:03:45.299 --> 00:03:48.520
The class of one is the second column

80
00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:51.410
and the class of two is the third.

81
00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.409
Is the class of one different

82
00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.601
from the class of four?

83
00:03:56.601 --> 00:03:56.966
No.

84
00:03:56.966 --> 00:03:59.524
Four is in the same column

85
00:03:59.524 --> 00:04:02.538
it is one class with many names.

86
00:04:02.629 --> 00:04:06.009
Two names give the same class exactly

87
00:04:06.100 --> 00:04:08.840
when the elements are related.

88
00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:13.703
This is true for every equivalence relation.

89
00:04:13.703 --> 00:04:16.234
First, no class is empty

90
00:04:16.234 --> 00:04:20.030
a is in its own class, by reflexivity.

91
00:04:20.030 --> 00:04:22.950
Second, if a is related to b

92
00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:26.260
the classes of a and b are equal.

93
00:04:26.610 --> 00:04:26.990
Why?

94
00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:29.460
Take x in the class of a.

95
00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.405
x is related to a, and a to b

96
00:04:32.405 --> 00:04:36.110
so by transitivity, x is related to b.

97
00:04:36.110 --> 00:04:39.530
Symmetry gives the other inclusion.

98
00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:43.584
So two classes are either equal or disjoint

99
00:04:43.584 --> 00:04:45.688
they never just overlap.

100
00:04:45.772 --> 00:04:47.792
And together the classes

101
00:04:47.877 --> 00:04:49.560
cover the whole set.

102
00:04:50.140 --> 00:04:54.042
Pieces like these have a name: a partition.

103
00:04:54.042 --> 00:04:58.124
The parts are nonempty, they do not overlap

104
00:04:58.124 --> 00:05:01.300
and together they make up the set.

105
00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:03.700
The converse holds too.

106
00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:07.711
Cut one to six into one four, two three six

107
00:05:07.711 --> 00:05:08.603
and five.

108
00:05:08.692 --> 00:05:11.188
Relate two numbers when they

109
00:05:11.277 --> 00:05:13.149
are in the same part.

110
00:05:13.149 --> 00:05:16.090
That is an equivalence relation.

111
00:05:16.440 --> 00:05:19.178
List the elements part by part

112
00:05:19.267 --> 00:05:21.740
and its grid is three blocks

113
00:05:21.828 --> 00:05:23.241
on the diagonal.

114
00:05:23.241 --> 00:05:26.156
Four pairs, plus nine, plus one

115
00:05:26.156 --> 00:05:27.569
fourteen pairs.

116
00:05:27.657 --> 00:05:29.866
So a four-element set has

117
00:05:29.954 --> 00:05:31.632
fifteen partitions

118
00:05:31.632 --> 00:05:34.724
and fifteen equivalence relations

119
00:05:34.724 --> 00:05:35.960
one for each.

120
00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:38.922
So the classes of an equivalence

121
00:05:38.996 --> 00:05:40.932
relation form a partition

122
00:05:41.006 --> 00:05:42.867
and every partition gives

123
00:05:42.941 --> 00:05:44.728
an equivalence relation.

124
00:05:44.728 --> 00:05:47.333
In code, equality must be one too

125
00:05:47.407 --> 00:05:49.194
but a floating-point NaN

126
00:05:49.268 --> 00:05:50.980
is not equal to itself.

127
00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:52.778
Try it yourself

128
00:05:52.868 --> 00:05:56.579
is a times b at least zero an equivalence

129
00:05:56.669 --> 00:05:58.931
relation on the integers?

130
00:05:58.931 --> 00:06:02.370
Next lecture: proof by contradiction.
